      "text": "To solve the system of equations\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe will use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of that variable in both equations equal.\n\nStep 1: Multiply the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5 to make the coefficients of \\(y\\) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign:\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22,\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Add the two resulting equations to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60) \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Substitute \\(x = -2\\) back into the first original equation to solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\nAdding 6 to both sides, we get:\n\\[\n-5y = -5 \\implies y = \\frac{-5}{-5} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is the ordered pair \\((x, y) = (-2, 1)\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
