      "text": "We will solve the system of equations using the method of elimination.\n\nFirst, let's label the equations for reference:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we need to make the coefficients of either \\(x\\) or \\(y\\) the same (or opposites) in both equations. Let's eliminate \\(y\\) by making the coefficients of \\(y\\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nWe start by multiplying Equation 1 by 2 and Equation 2 by 5 to make the coefficients of \\(y\\) equal in magnitude:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\\\\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22 \\quad \\text{(Equation 3)} \\\\\n35x + 10y = -60 \\quad \\text{(Equation 4)}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add Equation 3 and Equation 4 to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60)\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n41x = -82\n\\]\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = -2\\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(y\\). Let's use Equation 1:\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11\n\\]\nAdding 6 to both sides, we get:\n\\[\n-5y = -5\n\\]\nDividing both sides by -5, we get:\n\\[\ny = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\n\\]",
